#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046645} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536021} :activity/id #uuid "f0450c29-614d-584c-bd9d-e5e05615fc5b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049739 :topic/id #uuid "0fe4fee1-477c-5881-86c7-1921fd5fff4e" :topic/math-academy-id 3860} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536010 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373834 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ee8b8c0d-1ee4-5b74-98ae-a39f339006ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{K}{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373836} {:answer/id #uuid "f9fce73c-e33e-526e-ae9d-d604ddfe425e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}=-Ks" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373837} {:answer/id #uuid "de1fd09f-93d4-5865-b24c-0e86883ac345" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{K}{{s}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373838} {:answer/id #uuid "ec5e3a4a-13c7-547b-b482-926b18785c2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{K}{{s}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373839} {:answer/id #uuid "344b3dd6-8a8c-5b38-83e6-babf864485c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}=K{s}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373840}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373839} :answer-field/id #uuid "722e2706-48b9-573e-a5f7-472963351881" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373835}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "0936be88-f149-5a42-9d58-500c046543ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-143135" :question/problem "The area of a square decreases at a rate inversely proportional to its side length. Write down a differential equation describing the rate of change of the side length, $s(t),$ in this process. Assume that $K>0.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **area** of a square **decreases** at a rate **inversely proportional** to **its side length**.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *area $A$ of a square* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ *decreases* at a rate *inversely proportional its side length*. In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{k}{s}\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0.$\n\nWe wish to use this information to find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe area of a square is given by \n\n$$\nA={s}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this equation with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({s}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =2s\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{k}{s} \\\\ 2s\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{k}{s} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{k}{2{s}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now introduce a new constant:\n\n$$\nK=\\frac{k}{2}>0\n$$\n\nTherefore, our equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{K}{{s}^{2}}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "17e24c4d-fbe4-5267-b369-1dc13301b94c"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536021 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188915 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00359f0d-5c3b-50c1-a12e-737bc41221da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{′}(x)=k({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535436} {:answer/id #uuid "3c96294f-e5cd-511a-981b-9f5670bd9a23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{′}(x)=k{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535527} {:answer/id #uuid "7e897ffd-840e-5b35-92d6-f6d2bd060f6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{′}(x)=k\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535635} {:answer/id #uuid "ec89e486-9020-5137-9ccb-884bab47d61f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{′}(x)=k{x}^{2}{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535800} {:answer/id #uuid "f7ffbb76-ca6f-595c-aa26-024793132497" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{′}(x)=kxy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535813}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535635} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ce498ff-bed5-5a31-b8d4-a763ddd397a4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187566}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5deb4667-ba88-546b-92cb-11999248cbcf" :question/math-academy-id "q-38329" :question/problem "At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the derivative ${y}^{′}(x)$ is proportional to the distance from $P$ to the origin. What differential equation describes this curve?" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the **derivative** ${y}^{′}(x)$ is **proportional** to the **distance from $P$ to the origin**.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The *distance from $P(x,y)$ to the origin* is given by $\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.$\n\n- Since the *derivative* is *proportional* to the distance from $P$ to the origin, we have \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=k\\cdot \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a constant.\n\nSo, our differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=k\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "52e7e185-8a07-5891-8e9c-c1bf0de98ed5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536025}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536025 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535974 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5ca4cfd4-07ce-53b0-ac05-7ef895c2e528" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{K}{{s}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535582} {:answer/id #uuid "85d4c3b1-bd0d-5ede-ab88-6b4e89d39b8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}=K{s}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535648} {:answer/id #uuid "c0dfbcc4-c509-5a61-956d-8bd3f7248123" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{K}{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535729} {:answer/id #uuid "c85ccc6a-9e93-5f3b-a97f-d6c05f64a53d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}=K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535736} {:answer/id #uuid "de7decf8-37f7-5b67-adfa-4152ced74e30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}=Ks" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535777}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535582} :answer-field/id #uuid "ddc65ecf-2fc0-57a8-93c3-d6c4c4e692c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535914}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "9d65b7da-354a-5fe6-8886-5852de4be621" :question/math-academy-id "q-80488" :question/problem "The area of a square increases at a rate that's inversely proportional to its perimeter. Write down a differential equation describing the rate of change of the side length, $s(t),$ in this process. Assume that $K>0.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **area** of a square **increases** at a **rate** that's **inversely proportional** to its **perimeter**.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *area $A$ of a square* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ *increases* at a rate that's *inversely proportional* to its *perimeter.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{4s}\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0.$\n\nWe wish to use this information to find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe area of a square is given by \n\n$$\nA={s}^{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nDifferentiating this equation with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({s}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =2s\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{4s} \\\\ 2s\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{4s} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{(\\frac{k}{4s})}{2s} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{8{s}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now introduce a new constant:\n\n$$\nK=\\frac{k}{8}>0\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, our equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{K}{{s}^{2}}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "677b36df-44d3-546b-99e0-9f2b342dd41c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536040}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536040 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535968 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4eace44a-9542-5986-9bb4-4a121809282b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535551} {:answer/id #uuid "5184e724-e665-5cf4-9d18-27d961cdc95d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{k}{r}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535561} {:answer/id #uuid "537b4f55-d454-56dc-93a5-b86d06a5d655" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-kr" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535565} {:answer/id #uuid "71ee97c0-dcb3-5432-b6ad-3f697f647db0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{k}{{r}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535609} {:answer/id #uuid "a39bc14a-c886-535c-9199-d7ab15d57b18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-k{r}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535689}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535551} :answer-field/id #uuid "13b9af92-d428-5ecd-80c2-21192da8e8da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535837}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "907d5b64-98c1-552d-a43d-a699db06e688" :question/math-academy-id "q-119167" :question/problem "The volume of a sphere decreases at a rate that's proportional to its surface area. Write down a differential equation describing the rate of change of the radius, $r(t),$ in this process. Assume that $k>0.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*Its **volume decreases** at a **rate** that's **proportional** to its **surface area**.* \n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *volume $V$ of a sphere* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}$ *decreases* at a rate that's *proportional to its surface area $S.$* Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-kS,\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0.$ Now, using the fact that $S=4\\pi {r}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-4\\pi k{r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to use this information to find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe volume $V$ of a sphere is given by\n\n$$\nV=\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this equation with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =4\\pi {r}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =-4\\pi k{r}^{2} \\\\ 4\\pi {r}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-4\\pi k{r}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{4\\pi k{r}^{2}}{4\\pi {r}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-k.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "b67684ce-1284-5a67-8a4d-2617a5d04b2f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536048}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536048 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373799 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49a327b9-75a5-53f6-8b43-cf618bcf3524" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}=K{s}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373801} {:answer/id #uuid "1df99bf7-929a-5903-abcf-591bb526f29e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{K}{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373802} {:answer/id #uuid "5bbf909f-462f-5518-8013-a606f7cf41aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{K}{{s}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373803} {:answer/id #uuid "3d49d7ac-08ee-59ab-9b44-461517ebb9fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{K}{{s}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373804} {:answer/id #uuid "708d1932-73c6-5f7d-ae3b-5e1d6d9045b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}=K{s}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373805}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373803} :answer-field/id #uuid "20ed6ed5-0a52-5e29-8c5e-f028a3e0baf7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373800}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "deb34ff2-c563-5932-95e2-cff733776593" :question/math-academy-id "q-143125" :question/problem "The volume of a cube increases at a rate that's inversely proportional to the length of its edge. Write down a differential equation describing the rate of change of the edge length, $s(t),$ in this process. Assume that $K>0.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **volume** of a cube **increases** at a rate that's **inversely proportional** to the **length of its edge**.* \n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *volume $V$ of a cube* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}$ *increases* at a rate that's *inversely proportional to the length of its edge.* Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{s},\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0.$\n\nWe wish to use this information to find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe volume $V$ of a cube is given by\n\n$$\nV={s}^{3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this equation with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({s}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =3{s}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{k}{s} \\\\ 3{s}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{k}{s} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{k}{3{s}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now introduce a new constant:\n\n$$\nK=\\frac{k}{3}>0\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, our equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{K}{{s}^{3}}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "ee2e114c-cdf4-53db-b71c-1fd113ce8364" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536010}}] :activity/title "Further Modeling With First-Order ODEs" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535433}]}
